==Spanish==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|la|es}} {{m|la|centum}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|es}} {{m|ine-pro|*ḱm̥tóm}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{homophones|siento|lang=es}} {{qualifier|depending on region}}

===Number===
{{head|es|numeral|cardinal numeral||plural|cientos}}

# {{label|es|cardinal number}} [[one-hundred]] {{gloss|100}}

====Usage notes====
When used as an adjective before the modified noun, the [[apocopate]] form {{m|es|cien}} is used instead of {{m|es||ciento}}.

In composite numbers, the form {{m|en||cien}} is used before larger numbers components, {{m|es||ciento}} before smaller numbers.
: '''''cien''' mil pesos'' — “a hundred thousand pesos” ($100,000)
: '''''ciento''' noventa pesos'' — “a hundred ninety pesos” ($190)

When used substantively to indicate the number 100 itself, {{m|en||cien}} is more common in modern usage:
: ''los números de uno hasta el '''cien''''' — “the numbers from one to a hundred”
: ''hay dos '''cienes''' en el papel'' — “there are two hundreds on the paper” (two occurrences of the number one hundred)

In the indefinite sense, {{m|en||ciento}} is used:
: '''''cientos''' de pesos'' — “hundreds of pesos”

To indicate percentages, {{m|en||ciento}} is usually used, with regional exceptions, especially for 100%:
: ''cincuenta por '''ciento''''' — “fifty percent”
: '''''cien''' por '''cien''''' — “a hundred percent”
: '''''ciento''' por '''ciento''''' — “a hundred percent”

